<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Jibei</h1><p class="st-meta">206 BCE – 206 BCE · capital Boyang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A fragment of the old state of Qi given to Tian An in 206 BCE, destroyed within months when Tian Rong reunited Qi by force.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-an">Tian An</a>, a grandson of King Jian, the last king of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi</a>, captured several towns north of the Ji River during the war against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> and followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-julu">Julu</a>. In 206 BCE Xiang Yu made him King of Jibei, with his capital at Boyang in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Jibei was one of three kingdoms into which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> split Qi, alongside the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-jiaodong">Kingdom of Jiaodong</a> and Qi (206 BC), to weaken the powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a> clan. It lasted too short a time for any government to take shape.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-rong">Tian Rong</a>, the Qi strongman excluded from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu's</a> settlement, attacked the three kingdoms in 206 BCE, killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-an">Tian An</a> and reunited Qi under himself, provoking Xiang Yu's invasion of Qi.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-jibei.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Jibei" width="320" height="197"><figcaption>Image: Baomi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shandong_in_the_Qin_Dynasty.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>206 BCE – 206 BCE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Boyang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tian An</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jibei (Tian An)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-206&amp;focus=kingdom-of-jibei" aria-label="Kingdom of Jibei on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7643 1361 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Jibei lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7963 1617L7925 1091"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7925 1091h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7963 1617h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7963 1617h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Boyang. <a href="/atlas?year=-206&amp;focus=kingdom-of-jibei">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

